\subsection{Variables}

Due the hybrid nature of switched systems, we have two variable types: real-valued and discrete. As illustrated in \cref{fig:variable-diagram}, a real-valued variable is also a linear expression. A linear expression over a variable $\boldsymbol{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is a multiplication between a constant vector $\boldsymbol{h} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ and the variable plus a constant $c \in \mathbb{R}$: $\boldsymbol{h}^\intercal \boldsymbol{x} + c$. For example, variable $\boldsymbol{x} \in \mathbb{R}^2$ is also a vector of linear expressions: $\big((1, 0)^\intercal \boldsymbol{x} + 0.0, (0, 1)^\intercal \boldsymbol{x} + 0.0\big)$. A discrete variable is a variable which can assume a finite set of values. For example, in \cref{lst:light-dark}, $q$ is a discrete variable that have one valid value (i.e., $q \in \{ 0 \}$). 

We can declare a variable by the function $mk\_variable(size, dtype)$, where $dtype$ is one of of these two types: $float$ for real-valued variables, $int$ for discrete variables. If $dtype$ is $float$, $size$ is the variable dimension. If $dtype$ is $int$, $size$ is the cardinality of the variable set of values. We declare variables in our example in \cref{lst:light-dark} lines 4-6.

