	\end{center}
\end{figure}
Assuming the value $|\Delta \chi|\sim 1$ as the upper limit in the equation above, we can see that for the value $\alpha\sim 0.32$ and $n_{s}= 0.964$ the
first swampland condition is satisfied whenever
\begin{eqnarray}
\Delta U \sim  U\,\,\longrightarrow \,\,\frac{\Delta U}{U}\sim {\cal O}(1).
\end{eqnarray}
However, we can come up with stronger condition if we take $\alpha \ll 0.32 $. For example, using $\alpha=0.032$ we find $\frac{\Delta U}{U}\sim 0.07 \ll {\cal O}(1)$. The inequality of (\ref{delU}) is displayed in Fig.(\ref{sep4}). We can further consider the relation of Eq.(\ref{ns}). Hence we are able to write 
\begin{eqnarray}
\alpha\lesssim \frac{1}{4(3n_{s}-1)}\Big(6n_{s}+\sqrt{3r}-2-\sqrt{24 n_{s}+3 r-8}\Big). \label{ine}
\end{eqnarray}
