For case MO we use quadruple precision calculations with a relative and absolute timestepper tolerance of $10^{-18}$.  The mantle cools along approximate adiabats (constant $S$) until the adiabat intercepts the liquidus around 75 GPa (Fig.~\ref{fig:middle-out-1}a).  Then $\partial S/\partial r$ proceeds to decrease by 12 orders of magnitude below the liquidus as a consequence of $dS_{\rm liq}/dr$ switching sign from negative to positive.  Note the excellent agreement between the simplified and full models near the liquidus (compare Fig.~\ref{fig:semianal}b and Fig.~\ref{fig:middle-out-3}c, 0.4 kyr).  The large dynamic range of $\partial S/\partial r$ is the fundamental reason why quadruple precision is necessary for this case.  Where $dS_{\rm liq}/dr>0$, $\partial S/\partial r$ is driven to a small value ($\approx -10^{-13}$) to reduce the eddy diffusivity (Fig.~\ref{fig:middle-out-3}a) and hence the convective flux (Fig.~\ref{fig:middle-out-2}a).  This is because the majority of the total flux is accommodated by mixing (Eq.~\ref{eqn:Fmix}, Fig.~\ref{fig:middle-out-2}c) since $\partial \phi/\partial r<0$ in this region ($\kappa_h>0$).  This behaviour adheres to the insight gained from the simplified model (Section~\ref{sect:simplified}) where small negative $\partial S/\partial r$ is expected for $dS_{\rm liq}/dr>0$.  Despite the large variation in convective and mixing fluxes across the mantle, the total flux is near-constant and decreases steadily with time (Fig.~\ref{fig:middle-out-2}d).

