\section{Results}
\subsection{Morphology}
In Fig.~\ref{fig1}, we show our 6-cm ATCA image of SNR \mbox{{G1.9+0.3}}. From this image we define five distinct regions; the main ring/shell of the SNR, composed of an eastern (E) and two western (W1 \& W2) regions, and two possible blow-outs in the north (N) and in the south (S) directions beyond the main ring/shell. We emphasise that these two blow-outs have not been previously seen in any other images and further observation is required to determine their nature. 

Table~\ref{Tab1} lists the estimated flux densities of the 5 regions of \mbox{{G1.9+0.3}}\ as defined in Fig.~2. We estimate the total flux density of \mbox{{G1.9+0.3}}\ at 6-cm to be the sum of the flux densities of the main regions that compose this SNR (that is, E, W1 and W2, with the 'blow-out' regions N and S excluded) to be 1.545~Jy. This flux density is significantly smaller ($\sim$50\%) than the VLA estimates of \citet{2008MNRAS.387L..54G}. We can attribute this large difference to missing short spacings and poor {\it uv} covarge of the ATCA image.

