\subsection{Expansion}
In considering whether or not we should be able to realistically detect and measure any expansion between the 1985/2008 VLA images and the 1993 ATCA image, we must first consider the resolution of the ATCA image. With a FWHM of $4\arcsec\times4\arcsec$ the resolution of the image is 1\arcsec\ per pixel ie. our measurements are good to $\pm0\farcs5$. Assuming an approximate expansion rate of 0.65\% per year (as averaged over 23 years -- see \citet{2008MNRAS.387L..54G}), we would expect that the peak radius of \mbox{{G1.9+0.3}}\ should be $\sim31\farcs5$ in 1993, given a 1985 peak radius of 30\arcsec \citep{2008ApJ...680L..41R}. This corresponds to a difference of 1\farcs5 between 1985 and 1993 which is within our limit of resolution and thus detectable and measurable. 

Using the same method and centre (RA(J2000)=$17^\rmn{h}48^\rmn{m}$45\fs4, Dec(J2000)=$-27\degr10\arcmin06\arcsec$) described in \citet{2008MNRAS.387L..54G} we find that averaged over all angles, the shell profile of the 1993 ATCA image of \mbox{{G1.9+0.3}}\ peaks at a radius of 31\farcs75 (see Fig.~\ref{fig1} and Fig.~\ref{fig2}).  This represents an expansion in the range of 4.2\% to 7.5\% over eight years (or between $\sim$0.52\% and $\sim$0.94\% per year), once again using 30\arcsec\  \citep{2008ApJ...680L..41R} as the peak radius of \mbox{{G1.9+0.3}}\ in 1985. These findings support an approximate expansion rate of $\sim$0.65\% per year \citep{2008ApJ...680L..41R,2008MNRAS.387L..54G} and also supports an age $\la150$ years. We find that the velocity of the peak was $\sim8700~\pm~2400~\rmn{kms^{-1}}$ in 1993, assuming a distance to \mbox{{G1.9+0.3}}\ of 8.5~kpc.

From the radial profile (see Fig.~\ref{fig1} \& Fig.~\ref{fig2}) we adopt following diameter measurements corresponding to the epoch of the observation considered here (1993) for the purposes of future comparison studies:

\begin{itemize}
\item Inner diameter $\approx 43\arcsec$ (measured from where the profile in Fig. 2 rises above $\rmn{\sigma}=0.2~\rmn{mJy ~ beam^{-1}}$; 
\item Peak diameter $\approx 63\farcs5$ (the peak in Fig. 2); 
\item Outer diameter $\approx 80\arcsec$ (measured from where the profile in Fig. 2 falls to $\rmn{\sigma}=0.2~\rmn{mJy ~ beam^{-1}}$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Polarisation}
No polarisation was detected in the emission above the $1\%$ level: this result is not totally unexpected due to the use of ``snap-shot'' mode in this observation resulting in low $uv$ coverage. \citet{2008arXiv0806.1952M} suggested that \mbox{{G1.9+0.3}}\ is undergoing a period of magnetic field amplification: therefore, future radio-continuum observations should be able to detect polarisation.


\section{Conclusions}
We presented a 6-cm ATCA observation made in 1993 of SNR \mbox{{G1.9+0.3}}. We used this observation to confirm the estimates of expansion made by  \citet{2008ApJ...680L..41R} and \citet{2008MNRAS.387L..54G}. We identified three structures that compose the ring/shell of the SNR as well as two previously unseen possible blow-outs in the north and south directions. We were not able to detect any significant polarisation above the 1\% level.

The observation presented here (and the accompanying results) are significant in that, unlike most other  previously published radio observations of this SNR, this observation was made from a radio telescope located in the southern hemisphere: this geographic location is better suited for observations of low Declination sources (like \mbox{{G1.9+0.3}}) than telescopes located in the northern hemisphere. In fact, other published radio observations of this SNR that have been made with the VLA will suffers from distorted beam shapes that are inherent in VLA observations of objects located at low Declinations. In addition, this observation helps to add to our knowledge of this particular SNR (which is now known to be extremely young) which in turn will help us constrain models of the early evolution of SNRs."

We would like to emphasise the need for new higher resolution observations with better $uv$ coverage in order to obtain a more complete understanding of \mbox{{G1.9+0.3}}'s morphology (ie. possible blow-outs), as well as to more accurately determine the expansion rate, age and study its magnetic field structure. 

