ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$.  Although it is not clear how to define `simply connected' in noncommutative geometry, an analogue of the abelianization $H^1$ of the fundamental group is understood. We show that this noncommutative de Rham  cohomology $H^1_{d R}$ of $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ is essentially trivial when ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ obeys a certain antisymmetry-type condition which  we call `locally skew'. More precisely in this case
\[ H^1_{d R}(G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}})=k	heta, \]
the span of the inner generator  $	heta$  which has no classical analogue. Thus the classical part of the cohomology is trivial and the theorem says that our  `covering group' $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ in the locally skew case behaves geometrically like the universal cover of a topological group.  We will have shown in Section~4 that this applies to all simply laced Weyl groups, which now proves a conjecture which the first author proposed some time ago\cite{Ma:perm}, that $H^1_{d R}(S_n)=k	heta$ for the 2-cycles class. Our results settle the conjecture for all finite Weyl groups as well as a similar result $H^1_{d R}(D_{6m})=k	heta$ for the dihedral groups $D_{6m}$. We also show that ${\mathbb{Z}} P^1\cup {\mathbb{Z}} P^1$ generating $B_3$ is locally skew, so that the noncommutative cohomology here is also trivial when appropriately defined on this infinite group.

\section{IP quandles and its associated group}

Our background is the theory of braided-Lie algebras\cite{Ma:blie}. A braided Lie algebra is a coalgebra ${\mathcal{L}}}
ewcommand{\CR}{{\mathcal{R}}}
ewcommand{\CV}{{\mathcal{V}}$ in a braided category equipped with a `Lie bracket' $[\ ,\ ]$ subject to certain axioms. Associated to every braided Lie algebra is an induced map $	ilde\Psi:{\mathcal{L}}}
ewcommand{\CR}{{\mathcal{R}}}
ewcommand{\CV}{{\mathcal{V}}\otimes{\mathcal{L}}}
ewcommand{\CR}{{\mathcal{R}}}
ewcommand{\CV}{{\mathcal{V}}	o {\mathcal{L}}}
ewcommand{\CR}{{\mathcal{R}}}
ewcommand{\CV}{{\mathcal{V}}\otimes{\mathcal{L}}}
ewcommand{\CR}{{\mathcal{R}}}
ewcommand{\CV}{{\mathcal{V}}$ which obeys\cite{Ma:sol,Wam} the Yang-Baxter or braid relations, and we say that a braided-Lie algebra is regular when this map $	ilde\Psi$ is an isomorphism. In the case of ${\mathcal{L}}}
ewcommand{\CR}{{\mathcal{R}}}
ewcommand{\CV}{{\mathcal{V}}=k{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ in the category of $k$-vector spaces where ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ is a set and $k{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ its associated coalgebra, a regular braided-Lie algebra reduces to a rack. In general, associated to a braided-Lie algebra there is a braided enveloping bialgebra $U({\mathcal{L}}}
ewcommand{\CR}{{\mathcal{R}}}
ewcommand{\CV}{{\mathcal{V}})$ defined by relations of $	ilde\Psi$-commutativity. In the rack case the bialgebra becomes the algebra of a monoid with relations of $	ilde\Psi$ commutativity. Explicitly this monoid is
\[ S_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}=\<{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\>/ \  {}^ab a=ab,\qquad  orall a,b\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}.\]
Here  $	ilde\Psi(a,b)=({}^ab,a)$ is a map ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}	imes{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}	o {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}	imes {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ and makes $({\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}},	ilde\Psi)$ into a braided set in the sense of \cite{IvaMa}. As a result the monoid $S_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ has reasonable homological properties as an example of an M3-monoid in the notation of \cite{IvaMa}. Note that it is clear that ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subset S_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$, since the monoid has quadratic relations and hence is graded by degree, while ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ has degree~1.

 egin{definition} A left IP-quandle is a set ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ with an operation ${}^ab$ obeying (ef{rack}) and ${}^aa=a$ for all $a$, together with a permutation of ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ denoted $a\mapsto a^{-1}$ such that
\[  (a^{-1})^{-1}=a,\quad {}^a(b^{-1})=({}^ab)^{-1},\quad  {}^{a^{-1}}({}^{a}b)=b,\quad orall a,b\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}.\]
\end{definition}

Clearly this is necessarily a quandle since the third IP-axiom requires each ${}^a(\ )$ to be bijective. It also follows that ${}^{a^{-1}}a=a$ for all $a\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$. The notion is modelled after the notion of an IP-quasigroup and note that we do not require an identity. 

Next, it is easy to see that if ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ is an IP-quandle then $a^{-1}a$ is central in $S_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ for all $a\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$. Indeed, $a^{-1}ab=a^{-1}({}^ab)a={}^{a^{-1}}({}^ab)a^{-1}a=ba^{-1}a$. 


 egin{definition} Let ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ be an IP-quandle. We define the group  $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ as $S_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ modulo the further relations $a^{-1}a=e$ for all $a\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$. We say that the IP-quandle is {\em embeddable} if  ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subset S_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ descends to an inclusion ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subseteq G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\setminus\{e\}$. 
\end{definition}

Clearly $aba^{-1}={}^abaa^{-1}={}^ab$ in $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ for all $a,b\in{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$. Hence for an embeddable IP-quandle we have ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subseteq G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\setminus\{e\}$, closed under conjugation by $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ and clearly closed under inversion.  


  egin{example} Let ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}=\{a,b,c,a^{-1},b^{-1},c^{-1}\}$ with IP-quandle structure
 \[ {}^aa={}^bc={}^ca=a,\quad {}^ab={}^bb={}^cb=b,\quad {}^ac={}^ba={}^cc=c\]
 and corresponding properties for the inverses (for example ${}^a(c^{-1})=({}^ac)^{-1}=c^{-1}$ etc.) One can verify that the IP quandle axioms hold. However,  viewed in $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ we have
 $a=abb^{-1}={}^ab a b^{-1}=bab^{-1}={}^ba=c$ when viewed in $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$, so this IP-quandle is not embeddable. \end{example}

On the other hand, we will normally be interested in IP-quandles constructed from existing groups and in this case the following applies.

 egin{proposition}\label{coverexact} Let $G$ be a group and ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ an ad-stable generating subset ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subseteq G\setminus \{e\}$ closed under group inversion. Then ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ under conjugation and inversion is an embeddable IP-quandle, the canonical inclusion $i:{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\hookrightarrow G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}} \setminus \{e\}$ is a map of IP-quandles and covered by the  canonical group homomorphism $\pi:G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}	woheadrightarrow G$ so that $\pi\circ i=m id$. Moreover, its kernel $N$ defined by
\[ 1	o N	o G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}{ uildrel \pi\over 	o} G	o 1\]
is a central subgroup of $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$.   \end{proposition}
\proof  This is straightforward. (1) The relations defining $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ already hold in $G$, and ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ generates $G$ so the map which associates to $a\in{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subset S_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ its corresponding element $a\in G$,  is a surjection and descends to a group homomorphism $\pi:G	o G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$, as stated. The canonical map $i:{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}	o G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ likewise descending from ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subset S_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ composes with the surjection $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}	woheadrightarrow G$ to give the inclusion ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\hookrightarrow G$,  and hence is itself an injection. The image of $i$ cannot contain $e\in G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ as this would map under $\pi$ to $e\in G$ and $e
otin{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ when viewed in $G$. Moreover,  $i({}^ab)=i(a)i(b)i(a)^{-1}$, $i(a^{-1})=i(a)^{-1}$ by construction of $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$.  (2) If $n\mapsto e$  under $\pi$ then for all $a\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ we have $nan^{-1}\mapsto a$. Hence $nan^{-1}={}^na$ and $a$ agree as elements of ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ and hence of  $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$. Therefore $n\in N=\ker(\pi)$ commutes with all elements of ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subset G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$. Since $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ is generated by ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$, the subgroup $N$ is central. \endproof

In view of this we henceforth think of ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subseteq G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\setminus\{e\}$ and ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subseteq G\setminus \{e\}$ as sub IP-quandles without writing the map $i$ explicitly.

 egin{remark} In the setting of the previous proposition, $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ is universal in the sense that if $\phi: H	woheadrightarrow G$ is a group homomorphism and $j:{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\hookrightarrow H\setminus\{e\}$ is an inclusion of IP-quandles such that $\phi\circ j=m id$ then there is an induced group homomorphism $\psi$ such that
\[  egin{array}{ccc} G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}} & { uildrel \pi\over \longrightarrow} & G\ \psi\searrow & &
earrow\phi \ &H &\end{array}\]
commutes. 
\end{remark} \proof  Suppose $\phi:H	woheadrightarrow G$ and  $j:{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\hookrightarrow H\setminus\{e\}$ obeys $\phi\circ j=m id$ and $j({}^ab)=j(a)j(b)j(a)^{-1}$. We define $\psi(a)=j(a)$ and extend this as a homomorphism of groups. Here $\psi({}^a b a)=j({}^ab)j(a)=j(a)j(b)=\psi(ab)$ and $\psi(a^{-1})=\psi(a)^{-1}$. We have $\phi(\psi(a))=a$ for all $a\in{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$. Then $\phi(\psi(a_1\cdots a_n))=\pi(a_1\cdots a_n)$ and since ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ generates $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ this means that the diagram commutes. \endproof

 egin{remark} Since we can always take ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}=G\setminus\{e\}$ among other possibilities, Proposition~ef{coverexact} tells us that up to a central extension every group is `quadratic' in the sense of a set of generators such that the relations are of the form $aa^{-1}=e$ or quadratic, indeed with the quadratic relations obeying the braid or Yang-Baxter identity. \end{remark}

We will be interested in the following `skew-symmetry' condition. We think of IP quandles obeying this condition as being closest in some sense to a classical Lie algebra:
 egin{definition} Let ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ be an IP-quandle. We say that two elements $a,b\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ are {\em mutually skew} if  ${}^a b=({}^ba)^{-1}$. We say that ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ is {\em skew} if any two distinct elements are mutually skew. Consider the graph on ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ with an edge whenever two distinct elements are mutually skew. We say that ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ is {\em locally skew} if this graph is connected. \end{definition}

Note that from its definition the notion of mutually skew is a property of the unordered pair, i.e. of the subset $\{a,b\}\subseteq{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$. Also $\{a,a\}$ are mutually skew iff we have $a=a^{-1}$, while $\{a,a^{-1}\}$ are always mutually skew. 

 egin{lemma}\label{skewbraid} Let ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ be an embeddable IP-quandle and $a,b\in{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$. The following are equivalent
 egin{enumerate}
\item $a,b$ are mutually skew,
\item ${}^{b^{-1}}({}^a(b^{-1}))=a$,
\item ${}^{({}^{b^{-1}}a)}(b^{-1})=a$,
\item the elements $a,b^{-1}$ obey the braid relation $ab^{-1}a=b^{-1}ab^{-1}$  in $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$,
\item $a^{-1},b^{-1}$ are mutually skew,
\end{enumerate} and are equivalent to the same with $a,b$ interchanged. In this case
 egin{equation}\label{skewIP} {}^{({}^b a)}(a^{-1})=b\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
\proof (2)-(3) are elementary from the IP quandle axioms. For (4) we rearrange as $bab^{-1}=ab^{-1}a^{-1}$ which in view of the relations in $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ becomes ${}^ba={}^a{(b^{-1})}$ and the right hand side is $({}^a b)^{-1}$, so this condition is the same as (1). On the other hand (4) can also be rearranged as $a^{-1}b^{-1}a=b^{-1}ab$ which in view of the relations in $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ become ${}^{a^{-1}}(b^{-1})={}^{b^{-1}}a$ and the right hand side $({}^{b^{-1}}(a^{-1}))^{-1}$, so this condition is (5). 
Then if $a,b$ and $a^{-1},b^{-1}$ are both mutually skew we have ${}^{({}^ba)}a^{-1}={}^{({}^ab^{-1})}a^{-1}={}^a({}^{b^{-1}}a^{-1})={}^a({}^{a^{-1}}b)=b$. \endproof


Finally, we have been looking at left quandles. There is a parallel theory of right quandles in which we reflect the diagrams at the braided-Lie algebra level so that in our case a right quandle obeys 
\[ (a^b)^c=(a^c)^{(b^c)}\]
with $(\ )^b$ bijective and $a^a=a$ for all $a,b,c\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$. For a right quandle we have  $	ilde\Psi(a,b)=(b,a^b)$ and the relations of $S_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ are $ab=b(a^b)$. By definition the quandle is IP if  there is an involutive bijection denoted as inversion such that  $(a^{-1})^b=(a^b)^{-1}$ and $(a^b)^{b^{-1}}=a$ for all $a,b\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$. 

 egin{lemma} A left IP quandle ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ defines a right IP quandle on the same set with $a^b=({}^{b^{-1}}(a^{-1}))^{-1}$ such that  $(\ )^{-1}$ extends to an isomorphism of the associated groups. \end{lemma}
\proof  Regarding the data for a left quandle as a right one gives a monoid with the opposite product. The corresponding groups are then isomorphic via group inversion and at the quandle level this reduces to the relationship shown.  \endproof

We can similarly convert from right to left in the IP case. Clearly embeddability in one case is likewise equivalent to embeddabilty in the other.


\section{Finite covering groups}

We say that a group $G$ is a covering group if it has a conjugation-stable inversion-stable generating subset ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subseteq G\setminus\{e\}$, regarded as an IP-quandle, such that $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}	woheadrightarrow G$ is an isomorphism. This means that such a group has a presentation in the form \[ {}^ab a= ab,\quad aa^{-1}=e\quad  orall a,b\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\]
where ${}^ab=aba^{-1}$ is data extracted from the group but only this data is remembered in the form of an IP-quandle. We show in this section that many familiar finite groups are of this form.


 egin{theorem}  Every finite crystallographic reflection group (i.e. Weyl group)  is a covering group with respect to ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ the set of reflections. Moreover, the IP-quandle of reflections is locally skew {\em iff}  the associated Dynkin diagram is simply laced. \end{theorem}
\proof We choose a root system of Dynkin type appropriate to the Weyl group, i.e. a system of roots $\{\alpha\}$ in ${\mathbb{R}}^n$ for some $n$ with its standard inner product\cite{Hum}. We also choose simple roots $\{\alpha_i\}$. We do not use the full data here and different data can define the same group. Each root   defines a hyperplane reflection which we write as $r_\alpha(v)=v-  \<\check\alpha,v\> \alpha$ for $ v \in {\mathbb{R}}^n$, where $\check\alpha$ is the coroot of $\alpha$. The quandle ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ can then be identified with the set of roots modulo sign (or all the positive roots) and the IP-quandle structure and relations of $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ are represented by
\[{}^{r_\alpha}r_ eta=r_{r_\alpha( eta)},\quad  r_{r_\alpha( eta)}r_\alpha=r_\alpha r_ eta,\quad  r_\alpha^{-1}=r_\alpha.\]
 We use the notation $r_i$ for the reflection associated to the simple root $\alpha_i$. Using the relations of $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ only, we verify that we can recover all the Coxeter relations as follows. (1) If $\<\check\alpha_i,\alpha_j\>=0$ then ${}^{r_i}r_j=r_j$ and hence $r_jr_i=r_ir_j$. (2) If $\<\check\alpha_i,\alpha_j\>=\<\check\alpha_j,\alpha_i\>=-1$ then ${}^{r_i}r_j=r_{\alpha_i+\alpha_j}$ and hence $r_{\alpha_i+\alpha_j}r_i=r_ir_j$. By symmetry we have similarly $r_{\alpha_i+\alpha_j}r_j=r_jr_i$. Hence $r_ir_jr_i=r_{\alpha_i+\alpha_j}=r_jr_ir_j$ or $(r_ir_j)^3=1$. (3) If $\<\check\alpha_i,\alpha_j\>=-2$ and $\<\check\alpha_j,\alpha_i\>=-1$ we have ${}^{r_i}r_j=r_{\alpha_j+2\alpha_i}$ while ${}^{r_j}r_{\alpha_j+2\alpha_i}=r_{\alpha_j+2\alpha_i}$, hence $r_{\alpha_j+2\alpha_i}r_i=r_ir_j$ and $r_jr_{\alpha_j+2\alpha_i}=r_{\alpha_j+2\alpha_i}r_j$. Then $r_ir_jr_ir_j=r_{\alpha_j+2\alpha_i}r_j=r_jr_{\alpha_j+2\alpha_i}=r_jr_ir_jr_i$ or $(r_ir_j)^4=1$. (4) If $\<\check\alpha_i,\alpha_j\>=-3$ and $\<\check\alpha_j,\alpha_i\>=-1$ we have ${}^{r_i}r_j=r_{\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}$,  ${}^{r_j}r_{\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}=r_{2\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}$ and ${}^{r_i}r_{2\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}=r_{2\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}$, hence $r_ir_j=r_{\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}r_i$, $r_jr_{\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}=r_{2\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}r_j$ and  $r_ir_{2\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}=r_{2\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}r_i$.  Hence $r_ir_jr_ir_jr_ir_j=r_{\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}r_jr_ir_j=r_jr_{2\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}r_ir_j=r_jr_ir_{2\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}r_j=r_jr_ir_jr_{\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}=r_jr_ir_jr_ir_ir_{\alpha_j+3\alpha_i}=r_jr_ir_jr_ir_jr_i$ or $(r_ir_j)^6=1$. Hence $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ recovers the Weyl group. 

 In the simply laced case, the graph $\Gamma$ of mutually skew edges contains the Dynkin diagram $D$ itself -- two adjacent nodes are labeled by reflections obeying the braid relations hence by Lemma~ef{skewbraid} are mutually skew. We show that any reflection is connected by a path to this Dynkin diagram. Indeed, any root can be obtained by the action of the Weyl group from a simple root and hence is of the form $r_{i_k}r_{i_{k-1}}\cdots r_{i_1}(\alpha_i)$, say. We apply these simple reflections in turn and show that the accumulated subgraph  of $\Gamma$ remains connected. Thus, applying $r_{i_1}$ to $D$ gives a translate $r_{i_1}(D)$ of $D$ as a subgraph of $\Gamma$, because the Weyl group acts as a group automorphism by conjugation and hence two vertices are connected after the action of $r_{i_1}$ iff they  were before. The translate is connected to $D$ since at least one node, $\alpha_{i_1}$ itself, is invariant so lies in both $D$ and its translate. We write $\Gamma_1=D\cup r_{i_1}(D)$ for the combined connected subgraph. Now we apply $r_{i_2}$ to $\Gamma_1$ to obtain a subgraph $r_{i_2}(\Gamma_1)$ as its translate. This translate remains connected to $D$ since $\alpha_{i_2}\in D$ is invariant and so also lies in $r_{i_2}(\Gamma_1)$. We write $\Gamma_2=D\cup r_{i_2}(\Gamma_1)$ for the combined connected graph. Iterating this process with $\Gamma_{k+1}=D\cup r_{i_{k+1}}(\Gamma_{k})$ we arrive at a connected subgraph of $\Gamma$ which contains the element of the Weyl group that we began with as well as $D$. Hence the group is locally skew. 

Next we claim that if $r_\alpha,r_ eta$ are mutually skew then $\alpha, eta$ have the same length. Hence in the non-simply laced case the graph of mutually skew edges must have different components and the quandle is not locally skew. Indeed,  $r_{\alpha}$ and $r_{ eta}$ being mutually skew means 
$r_\alpha( eta)=\pm r_ eta(\alpha)$, i.e. $ eta-\alpha\<\check\alpha, eta\>=\alpha- eta\<\check eta,\alpha\>$ or $ eta-\alpha\<\check\alpha, eta\>=-\alpha+ eta\<\check eta,\alpha\>$. If $\alpha
e\pm eta$ we conclude in the first case that $r_\alpha( eta)=\alpha+ eta=r_ eta(\alpha)$ and in the second case that $r_\alpha( eta)= eta-\alpha=-r_ eta(\alpha)$. In the first case, since $\alpha=r_\alpha( eta)- eta$, we deduce that $r_ eta(\alpha)=r_ eta(r_\alpha( eta)- eta)=r_ eta r_\alpha( eta)+ eta=\alpha+ eta$ hence $r_ eta r_\alpha( eta)=\alpha$. Hence $\alpha$ has the same length as $ eta$ as reflections do not change length. There is a similar argument in the other case.  \endproof


 egin{example} Let ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subset S_n$ be the conjugacy class of 2-cycles. Clearly 
\[ {}^{(ij)}(kl)=(kl),\quad  {}^{(ij)}(ij)=(ij),\quad  {}^{(ij)}(jk)=(ik)\]
for disjoint $i,j,k$ and $(ij)^{-1}=(ij)$. Hence ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ is a skew IP-quandle if $n\le 3$ and clearly a locally skew one if $n>3$. The relations of $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ are 
\[ (ij)(kl)=(kl)(ij),\quad (ik)(ij)=(ij)(jk),\quad (ij)^2=e\]
for disjoint $i,j,k$. These imply the usual Coxeter relations since $(i,i+1)(i+1,i+2)(i,i+1)=(i,i+2)(i,i+1)^2=(i,i+2)(i+1,i+2)^2=(i+1,i+2)(i,i+1)(i+1,i+2)$ using only the relations above. Hence the relations stated form a presentation of $S_n$  and hence $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
