 egin{proposition} The dihedral groups $D_{2n}={\mathbb{Z}}_ntimes{\mathbb{Z}}_2$ are  covering groups with respect to their generating sets ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}={\mathbb{Z}}_nx$, where $x$ is the generator of ${\mathbb{Z}}_2$. Moreover, ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ is skew iff $n=1,3$ and for $n>1$ is locally skew iff $n$ is divisible by 3. \end{proposition}
\proof Here $D_{2n}$ has generators $a,x$ and relations $a^n=x^2=e$, $xa=a^{-1}x$ and ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}={\mathbb{Z}}_nx$ generates (this is a $n$ conjugacy class when $n$ is odd and a sum of two classes when $n$ is even). Writing its members $e_i=a^ix$ where $i=0,\cdots,n-1$ we have 
\[ {}^{e_i}e_j=e_{2i-j},\quad i,j\in {\mathbb{Z}}_n\]
where the indices are treated modulo $n$. Hence the IP-quandle here is skew iff $n=1,3$. More generally $\{e_i,e_j\}$ are mutually skew iff $n$ divides $3(i-j)$. This means that unless $n=3m$ (so $D_{6m}$) we have no distinct mutually-skew pairs, while for $D_{6m}$ we have for example $\{e_i,e_{i+1}\}$ are mutually skew for all $i\in{\mathbb{Z}}_n$ and hence $D_{6m}$ is locally skew. The relations of $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$, where $e_i$ are regarded abstractly as generators, are
\[ e_{i-j}e_i=e_ie_{i+j},\quad e_i^2=e,\quad  orall\ i,j\in{\mathbb{Z}}_n\]
Conversely given this group $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ we let $a=e_1e_0=e_1e_2=\cdots=e_{n-1}e_{n-2}=e_0e_{n-1}$ by repeatedly using the relations. We then have $a^i=e_{i}e_0=e_{i+1}e_1=\cdots =e_{i+n-1}e_{n-1}$ as we prove by induction: suppose true for $a^{i-1}$ then $a^i=aa^{i-1}=(e_{j+1}e_j)(e_j e_{j-i+1})=e_{j+1}e_{j+1-i}$ for all $j$ under our induction hypothesis.  In particular, $a^n=e_0e_0=e$. We also let $x=e_0$ so that $x^2=e$ and $xa=e_0e_1e_0=e_1e_2e_0=a^{-1}x$. Hence all the relations of $D_{2n}$ are recovered and $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}=D_{2n}$. 
\endproof

This includes $D_2={\mathbb{Z}}_2$ and $D_4={\mathbb{Z}}_2	imes{\mathbb{Z}}_2$ as covering groups with respect to the stated ${\mathcal{C}}}
