\section{Covering group of  $SL_2({\mathbb{Z}})$}

We are not limited to finite groups and quandles and in this section we describe an infinitely generated example based on  $SL_2({\mathbb{Z}})$. We let ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}={\mathbb{Z}} P^1\cup{\mathbb{Z}} P^1=\{e_{\vec a}, e^{-1}_{\vec a}\}$ where $\vec a\in \{(a,c)\in {\mathbb{Z}}^2\ |\ (a,c)=1\}/{\mathbb{Z}}_2$ (coprime integers modulo ${\mathbb{Z}}_2$ acting by -1 and noting that $(0,1)=(1,0)=1$ so these are included) and
\[ e_{ (a,c)}=\left(
 egin{array}{cc}
 1-a c & a^2 \
 -c^2 & 1+a c
\end{array}
ight),\quad e_{(a,c)}^{-1}=\left(
 egin{array}{cc}
 1+a c & -a^2 \
 c^2 & 1-a c
\end{array}
ight).\]
This the union of the conjugacy classes of $e_{(1,0)}= egin{pmatrix}  1&1\cr 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}$ and $e^{-1}_{(0,1)}= egin{pmatrix}1&0\cr 1&1\end{pmatrix}$. We have an IP-quandle with 
\[ {}^{e_{\vec a}}e_{\vec b}=e_{\vec a|{{\vec a}\atop{\vec b}}|+\vec b},\quad  {}^{e_{\vec a}}e^{-1}_{\vec b}=e^{-1}_{\vec a|{{\vec a}\atop{\vec b}}|+\vec b},\quad {}^{e^{-1}_{\vec a}}e_{\vec b}=e_{-\vec a|{{\vec a}\atop{\vec b}}|+\vec b},\quad  {}^{e^{-1}_{\vec a}}e^{-1}_{\vec b}=e^{-1}_{-\vec a|{{\vec a}\atop{\vec b}}|+\vec b}\]
and the associated group $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}	woheadrightarrow SL_2({\mathbb{Z}})$ with relations 
\[ e_{\vec a}e_{\vec b}=e_{\vec a|{{\vec a}\atop{\vec b}}|+\vec b}e_{\vec a}. \]
Here $|\ |$ denotes determinant. It is easy to see that $\{e_{\vec a},e_{\vec b}\}$ are never mutually skew, and $\{e_{\vec a},e^{-1}_{\vec b}\}$ are mutually skew iff either $\vec a=\pm \vec b$ (which means $\{e_{\vec a},e^{-1}_{\vec a}\}$) or $|{\vec a\atop \vec b}|=\pm 1$ (it is always possible to find $\vec b$ for a given $\vec a$ obeying this by the Bezout identity). Each such pair then gives by Lemma~ef{skewbraid} a homomorphic image of the braid group $B_3$ in $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ via $e_{\vec a}$ and $e_{\vec b}$. For example $e_{(1,0)}$, $e^{-1}_{(0,1)}$ are mutually  skew and hence give such a homomorphic image by $e_{(1,0)},e_{(0,1)}$. 

 egin{proposition} The IP quandle ${\mathbb{Z}} P^1\cup{\mathbb{Z}} P^1$ is locally skew. Moreover, the associated group $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ is isomorphic to $B_3$, the braid group on 3 strands.
\end{proposition}
\proof (1) We construct a path between $e_{\vec a}$ and $e^{-1}_{\vec b}$  or $e_{\vec b}$ by steps of the form $\{e_{\vec a},e^{-1}_{\vec a_1}\}, \{e^{-1}_{\vec a_1},e_{\vec a_2}\},\cdots, \{e_{\vec a_n},e^{-1}_{\vec b}\}$ in the first case and similarly in the other case. We can insert a pair $\{e_{\vec b},e_{\vec b}^{-1}\}$ as necessary to make our sequence of this form. Then by the above observations, we need to find coprime vectors in ${\mathbb{Z}}^2$ so that adjacent members of  the sequence $\vec a, \vec a_1,\cdots,\vec a_n,\vec b$ have mutual determinant $\pm 1$. This means that from the origin they form a triangle of area 1/2. This can be achieved as follows: form a triangle from the origin with sides $\vec a,\vec b$. We then triangulate the interior of this with vertices taken from ${\mathbb{Z}}^2$. This can always be achieved by induction (pick any interior vertex to make a triangle and then triangulate the remainder). Then starting at $\vec a$, the desired sequence is provided by the edge vectors in turn rotating about the origin until reaching $\vec b$. Each triangle formed from the adjacent edges has no interior points and hence area 1/2 by Pick's theorem.  (2) Observe first that for any group $G$ and ad-stable inversion stable generating set ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}\subseteq G\setminus \{e\}$, if a subset $S\subseteq{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ generates $G$  as a group and generates ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ as an ad-stable subset then $S$ also generates $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$. This is because if $a\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ we realise it as $a=(s_1\cdots s_{n-1})s_n(s_1\cdots s_{n-1})^{-1}= {}^{s_1}({}^{s_2}(\cdots({}^{s_{n-1}}s_n)\cdots))$ for some generators. Viewing this in $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ we obtain $a$ as a series of conjugations by the $s_i$. We can then use that ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ generates. Now in our case the elements  $e_{(1,0)}$ and $e^{-1}_{(0,1)}$ generate $SL_2({\mathbb{Z}})$ and hence $G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ and hence the map from $B_3$ described above is surjective. 
The map is injective because the kernel of the map $B_3	o SL_2({\mathbb{Z}})$ is known to be ${\mathbb{Z}}$ generated by the element $(b_1b_2)^{6}$ where $b_1,b_2$ are the standard generating braids. Hence any elements  in the kernel of the factoring map $B_3	o G_{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ are powers of $(b_1b_2)^{6}$. But  $(e_{(0,1)}e_{(1,0)})^{6}$ has infinite order because among  the $e_{\vec a}$ the relations are all quadratic and hence any product of them can never be the identity.   \endproof

There is an identical story with $PSL_2({\mathbb{Z}})$. Because elements of ${\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$ above all have trace $2$ they are all distinct in $PSL_2({\mathbb{Z}})$, so it has the same `Lie algebra'. 


