The general notion of differential graded algebras over algebras is one of the components of noncommutative geometry\cite{Con}. In particular,  left and right translation covariant or `bicovariant' differentials on Hopf algebras have been much studied since their formulation in \cite{Wor}. The specialisation to finite groups is also well-known and we refer to \cite{Ma:cdo,Ma:prim} for an introduction. Briefly, let $G$ be a finite group and $k(G)$ be the algebra of functions. Bicovariant  noncommutative differential calculi $\Omega^1(k(G))$ correspond to Cayley digraphs where arrows are of the form $x	o xa$ for $a\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}$,  a fixed ad-stable subset of $G\setminus\{e\}$. Here $\Lambda^1={m span}_k\{\omega_a\}_{a\in{\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}}$  is the space of left-invariant 1-forms and in terms of the Kronecker delta-functions the basis 1-forms are given by $\omega_a=\sum_{x\in G}\delta_x{m d}\delta_{xa}$. We have 
\[ \omega_a.f=R_a(f)\omega_a,\quad {m d}
f=\sum_{a\in {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
ewcommand{\CA}{{\mathcal{A}}}{\partial}^a(f)\omega_a,\quad orall f\in k(G),\]
where the finite partial derivative is ${\partial}^a=R_a-m id$ in terms of right translation $R_a(f)=f((\ )a)$.  The calculus is inner with $	heta=\sum_a\omega_a$ in the sense that ${m d} f=	heta f - f	heta$ for all $f\in k(G)$, and is connected iff $ {\mathcal{C}}}
ewcommand{\CN}{{\mathcal{N}}}
