	\subsection{Image Restoration}
	By introducing corresponding data augmentations to the input images during the training phase, our GAN inversion method can be easily extended to perform a number of image restoration tasks, such as: colorization, inpainting, super resolution. Here we qualitatively compare our method with pSp \cite{Richardson2020EncodingIS} on these tasks, both of which use the same data augmentations. Figure \ref{fig:restorationandtranslation} shows the visual comparison of our method with pSp on these three tasks.

	\vspace{0.5em}
	\noindent\textbf{Colorization.} For colorization task, we use the color image as the target and the corresponding grayscale image as input when training the network. Compared with pSp \cite{Richardson2020EncodingIS}, our approach accomplishes reasonable coloring for the grayscale image while ensuring consistency in identity and local details (\eg, glasses).


	\vspace{0.5em}
	\noindent\textbf{Inpainting.} To perform inpainting using the GAN inversion framework, we employ a degraded transformation to the input image, in which a region is randomly selected and the pixel value of that region is set to $ 0 $ during the training process. As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:restorationandtranslation}, our method achieves reasonable filling of unknown regions while keeping the known pixel values unchanged as much as possible.

	\vspace{0.5em}
	\noindent\textbf{Super Resolution.} Following the data augmentation method of pSp, we randomly downsample the input image by $ \times1 $, $ \times2 $, $ \times4 $, $ \times8 $, and $ \times16 $ and use the original resolution image as the target during training. Compared to pSp, our results are more realistic and with better facial details, \eg, the eyes in the shown example are more faithfully aligned with the low-resolution image.


	\subsection{Image Translation}
