In the absence of detection, we set upper limits on the rate of IMBH mergers at the 90\% confidence level by using the loudest event statistic~\cite{Biswas:2007vn}:
\begin{equation}
\label{eqn:rate}
R_{90\%} = \frac{2.3}{\nu(\textrm{FAD}^{\star})}\,.
\end{equation}
The $\nu(\textrm{FAD}^{\star})$ is the time-volume productivity of the search calculated at the FAD rate of the first ranked event (the top event in Table \ref{tbl:events}). The rate density upper limits calculated in a binning of the component masses are presented in Figure \ref{fig:rate_upper_limit}. The upper limit for the combined search, averaged over all masses, is estimated to be 0.9~Mpc${}^{-3}$Myr${}^{-1}\,$. In the most sensitive bin, the rate limit is nearly an order of magnitude greater than for the overall search at 0.13~Mpc${}^{-3}$Myr${}^{-1}\,$. 

Since globular clusters are the most likely hosts of IMBHs, we convert our overall search upper limit into an astrophysical density of 3\e{3} GC${}^{-1}$Gyr${}^{-1}\,$. This rate is still few orders of magnitude above the predictions for IMBH-IMBH rates in~\cite{ratesdoc}. It should be noted, however, that the predicted astrophysical rates are very uncertain due to the lack of knowledge of the distribution and formation of IMBH sources.

