For each of these three observation functions, we present the estimator with the optimal hypothesis space, the 2-Wasserstein distance in prediction and the convergence of the estimator in $L^2(\widebar \rho_T^L)$ (see Section \ref{sec:num-settings} for details).  

  \textbf{Sine function:}  Fig.~\ref{fig:DW-sine}(a) shows the estimator with degree-1 B-spline basis with dimension $n=9$ for $M=10^6$. 
